{"id":"cluster-administration-admission-webhooks-good-practices","corpus":"k8s","title":"Admission Webhook Good Practices","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/admission-webhooks-good-practices/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Admission Webhook Good Practices"},{"kind":"p","text":"Recommendations for designing and deploying admission webhooks in Kubernetes."},{"kind":"p","text":"This page provides good practices and considerations when designing admission webhooks in Kubernetes. This information is intended for cluster operators who run admission webhook servers or third-party applications that modify or validate your API requests."},{"kind":"p","text":"Before reading this page, ensure that you're familiar with the following concepts:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Admission controllers"},{"kind":"li","text":"Admission webhooks"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Importance of good webhook design"},{"kind":"p","text":"Admission control occurs when any create, update, or delete request is sent to the Kubernetes API. Admission controllers intercept requests that match specific criteria that you define. These requests are then sent to mutating admission webhooks or validating admission webhooks. These webhooks are often written to ensure that specific fields in object specifications exist or have specific allowed values."},{"kind":"p","text":"Webhooks are a powerful mechanism to extend the Kubernetes API. Badly-designed webhooks often result in workload disruptions because of how much control the webhooks have over objects in the cluster. Like other API extension mechanisms, webhooks are challenging to test at scale for compatibility with all of your workloads, other webhooks, add-ons, and plugins."},{"kind":"p","text":"Additionally, with every release, Kubernetes adds or modifies the API with new features, feature promotions to beta or stable status, and deprecations. Even stable Kubernetes APIs are likely to change. For example, the Pod API changed in v1.29 to add the Sidecar containers feature. While it's rare for a Kubernetes object to enter a broken state because of a new Kubernetes API, webhooks that worked as expected with earlier versions of an API might not be able to reconcile more recent changes to that API. This can result in unexpected behavior after you upgrade your clusters to newer versions."},{"kind":"p","text":"This page describes common webhook failure scenarios and how to avoid them by cautiously and thoughtfully designing and implementing your webhooks."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Identify whether you use admission webhooks"},{"kind":"p","text":"Even if you don't run your own admission webhooks, some third-party applications that you run in your clusters might use mutating or validating admission webhooks."},{"kind":"p","text":"To check whether your cluster has any mutating admission webhooks, run the following command:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output lists any mutating admission controllers in the cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"To check whether your cluster has any validating admission webhooks, run the following command:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output lists any validating admission controllers in the cluster."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Choose an admission control mechanism"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes includes multiple admission control and policy enforcement options. Knowing when to use a specific option can help you to improve latency and performance, reduce management overhead, and avoid issues during version upgrades. The following table describes the mechanisms that let you mutate or validate resources during admission:"},{"kind":"p","text":"In general, use webhook admission control when you want an extensible way to declare or configure the logic. Use built-in CEL-based admission control when you want to declare simpler logic without the overhead of running a webhook server. The Kubernetes project recommends that you use CEL-based admission control when possible."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Use built-in validation and defaulting for CustomResourceDefinitions"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you use CustomResourceDefinitions, don't use admission webhooks to validate values in CustomResource specifications or to set default values for fields. Kubernetes lets you define validation rules and default field values when you create CustomResourceDefinitions."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn more, see the following resources:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Validation rules"},{"kind":"li","text":"Defaulting"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Performance and latency"},{"kind":"p","text":"This section describes recommendations for improving performance and reducing latency. In summary, these are as follows:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Consolidate webhooks and limit the number of API calls per webhook."},{"kind":"li","text":"Use audit logs to check for webhooks that repeatedly do the same action."},{"kind":"li","text":"Use load balancing for webhook availability."},{"kind":"li","text":"Set a small timeout value for each webhook."},{"kind":"li","text":"Consider cluster availability needs during webhook design."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Design admission webhooks for low latency"},{"kind":"p","text":"Mutating admission webhooks are called in sequence. Depending on the mutating webhook setup, some webhooks might be called multiple times. Every mutating webhook call adds latency to the admission process. This is unlike validating webhooks, which get called in parallel."},{"kind":"p","text":"When designing your mutating webhooks, consider your latency requirements and tolerance. The more mutating webhooks there are in your cluster, the greater the chance of latency increases."},{"kind":"p","text":"Consider the following to reduce latency:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Consolidate webhooks that perform a similar mutation on different objects."},{"kind":"li","text":"Reduce the number of API calls made in the mutating webhook server logic."}],"lemmas":{"admissions":["admission","admissions"],"goods":["good","goods"],"best":["best","good"],"better":["better","good"],"practices":["practice","practices"],"practiced":["practiced","practice"],"practicing":["practicing","practice"],"recommendations":["recommendation","recommendations"],"designing":["designing","design"],"designings":["designing","designings"],"designs":["design","designs"],"deploying":["deploying","deploy"],"deployings":["deployings","deploying"],"deploys":["deploys","deploy"],"deployed":["deployed","deploy"],"webhooks":["webhooks","webhook"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"provides":["provides","provide"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"considerations":["consideration","considerations"],"whens":["whens","when"],"intended":["intended","intend"],"intendeds":["intendeds","intended"],"intends":["intends","intend"],"intending":["intending","intend"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"clustered":["clustered","cluster"],"operators":["operator","operators"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"servers":["server","servers"],"thirds":["third","thirds"],"parties":["party","parties"],"partying":["partying","party"],"applications":["application","applications"],"modifying":["modifying","modify"],"modified":["modified","modify"],"validates":["validates","validate"],"validated":["validated","validate"],"requests":["request","requests"],"requesting":["requesting","request"],"requested":["requested","request"],"reading":["reading","read"],"readings":["reading","readings"],"reads":["read","reads"],"ensures":["ensures","ensure"],"ensured":["ensured","ensure"],"ensuring":["ensuring","ensure"],"you'res":["you'res","you're"],"familiars":["familiar","familiars"],"following":["following","follow"],"followings":["following","followings"],"followed":["followed","follow"],"concepts":["concept","conc